From 956920434413fbd972553dfbf9054df5469b75da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:40:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A search leaves the blocks it finds reading whole

Three searches read their rows from blocks_fts with snippet() or
highlight() in place of the block's fields: the full-text search's FTS
route (fullTextSearchByFTSInBox, which a keyword search takes when it
doesn't search paths and a query-syntax search always takes), the
block-ref search typed after (( (fullTextSearchRefBlockInBox), and the
highlighting of a page opened from a search (highlightByFTSInBox). Their
rows went through scanBlockRows, which stores every row in SQL's block
cache, stripping search marks from Content only. GetBlock and GetBlocks read
that cache first, so until the block changed or the cache was cleared, a
found block read cut to its snippet, or with search marks in its name,
alias, memo, tag or attributes, wherever SiYuan reads a block by id: ref
text in exports and templates, and the backlinks panel, among them.

The two routes those searches use, SelectBlocksRawStmtArgsInBox and
SelectBlocksRawStmtNoParseInBox, get NoCache twins that scan rows without
storing them, through scanBlockRowsNoCache, and the three searches use
them. Results are built from the rows returned, so nothing they show
changes. The default keyword search with paths, and multi-word searches,
already read rows as maps.

A statement written outside SiYuan's code can select the same in place of
a block's fields, so every route that runs one reads its rows that way
too: an embed block's query, wherever SiYuan runs it (the editor's embed
search, the embed content it indexes and publishes, rendering, exports, a
page's statistics, and the assets of embedded blocks), the full-text
search's SQL method, and templates' queryBlocks. The route decides, not
the statement: SelectBlocksRawStmt, SelectBlocksRawStmtInBox,
SelectBlocksRawStmtInBoxContext, SelectBlocksRawStmtBoundedInBoxContext
and SelectBlocksRawStmtNoParse get NoCache twins, each pair sharing one
body; and searchBySQLInBoxContext takes its select from its caller, so
SiYuan's own lookups by id keep caching. What each route shows doesn't
change, and every read of blocks by a statement SiYuan builds caches as
before.

Its Go test is TestSearchLeavesBlocksWhole, in search_cache_fts5_test.go,
on a page holding a paragraph of 7,896 characters named "needle list": a
keyword search without paths, a query-syntax search, a block-ref search,
and opening the page with the query needle; then an embed block query and
an SQL-method search whose statements select snippet() in place of
content, and two template queries selecting highlight() and snippet() in
its place, each checking it shows what it did before; then GetBlock of the
page and the paragraph. Without the patch, on v3.8.7 with 0001 to 0008
applied, the first two left the paragraph 515 characters long and the
block-ref search 67, all three with search marks in its name, and opening
the page left the marks; without the routes for statements written outside
SiYuan's code, the embed block query, the SQL-method search and the
template query selecting snippet() left it 18 characters long and the one
selecting highlight() left it marked up. Without any one of the three
searches' changes, or the embed search's, the SQL method's or
queryBlocks', only its own cases fail; the other routes of embed blocks
have no case of their own.

Still open: SQL's block cache changes before the SQL commit, so a read in
that moment can cache the old row.
